nodecode-telemetry is a rewrite of nujovich's nujovich/hermes-telemetry:

  https://github.com/nujovich/hermes-telemetry
  commit 872eadc4b1e6797bc72c8eed3daf54f199d2bed5
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 nujovich (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's hooks, which recorded one row per provider round
(tokens in, out, cache read and write, reasoning, the cost the core priced,
the model and provider) and one row per tool call (name, wall time, whether it
failed), and its one gate, which refuses a tool call once a daily, monthly or
per-session ceiling is spent. The rewrite keeps that shape on Nodecode's own
seams: the :frame point, where turn.usage already carries the round's token
counts and priced cost and turn.provider_request carries its time to first
token and stream duration; the :tool around hook, which both journals a call
and is the one place a call can be refused before it runs; and the :round
point, where the over-budget warning rides into the next request. The ledger
is a JSON line file under the home, read and folded at start, and the caps
are the cell's own section, so /budget set writes the shared config instead
of a second budget file.

Left out, and why: the OpenRouter price-list fetch and the whole local price
catalogue with its drift and free-tier detection, because Nodecode's core
already prices every round and the fact carries cost_known and cost_usd; the
SQLite schema and its migrations (a line-oriented ledger answers the same
windows and never needs migrating); the per-cron-job and per-sender budget
scopes, folded into one per-session cap, since a cron fire and an inbound
message are both just sessions here; the cron job's pause on a hard breach,
which per-session refusal already covers; the standalone dashboard server
(it binds its own port with no auth, and Nodecode's gateway already serves a
usage view); the subagent_* and session-finalize hooks, which carry nothing
the row needs; and the first-load setup wizard, since the section's defaults
answer without one.

